{"author":{"id":"746a4d30afc20b78119cb8ed7c194f988174fc252f0e9d9eb92022244ef86db3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1782341433,"nonce":"4PA0KeWK/z15Zc9ZfjHY1NN2ARg=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOK4fCvM8AAAABGnhbdg","github-url":"https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/326","origin":"github"},"title":"docs(bizzyboat_power): add empirical speed → power/endurance/range curve (pooled lake surveys); refine range-optimal guidance","message":"## Summary\n\nUpdate **`bizzyboat_project11/docs/bizzyboat_power.md`** with an empirical\n**speed → power / endurance / range** characterization derived from the three\nMassabesic lake surveys (2026-06-22, 06-23, 06-24, ~14 h of steady straight-line\nrunning pooled). This refines the current \"Drive efficiently\" guidance and adds an\n**endurance** dimension for time-constrained planning.\n\nThis came out of a cross-deployment power-usage comparison of the 2026-06-24\ndeployment (no agent logging that day; analyzed from the standard bag via the\n`bag_analysis` `bag_to_sqlite` → coulomb-count pipeline, GPS dock-departure/return\nwindow since the boat is dock-tied at the lake).\n\n## Why (what's new / what it corrects)\n\nThe doc's current **Drive efficiently** section says *\"survey speed is already near\nrange-optimal (~3–3.5 kt)\"*. The pooled data shows that's only half-right:\n\n- The **range plateau is ~2.0–2.9 kt** (~31–33 statute mi on a full pack). **3.5 kt —\n  the historical most-used cruise (~8 of the 14 steady hours) — is already past the\n  plateau at ~23 mi (≈ −25 % range).**\n- There's a clear **drag step at ~3.2 kt**: current jumps ~35 A → ~46 A between 3.1\n  and 3.5 kt.\n- Backing the standard cruise off from **3.5 → 3.0 kt** costs ~8 % speed but buys\n  **+1.9 h endurance (5.9 → 7.8 h)** and **+5 mi range**, so **~2.9–3.0 kt is the\n  all-around operating point**; only go to 3.5+ kt when the clock is the hard limit.\n\nMethod validation: coulomb-quad on 06-23 = 273.9 Ah over the in-water window and the\npack (273 Ah nameplate) actually emptied; endurance derived as 273 Ah ÷ draw predicts\n6.2 h at that day's 44 A avg, matching its measured underway time.\n\n## Proposed table (statute miles; full 273 Ah pack; current incl. ~8 A hotel; ±~30 % model)\n\n| Speed | Current | Endurance | Range (mi) | mi/kWh |\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| 2.0 kt | 20 A | ~13.5 h | 31 | 4.6 |\n| 2.5 kt | 24 A | ~11.5 h | 32 | 4.5 |\n| 2.9 kt | 29 A | ~9.3 h | 31 | 4.2 |\n| 3.1 kt | 35 A | ~7.8 h | 28 | 3.8 |\n| 3.5 kt (usual) | 46 A | ~5.9 h | 23 | 3.35 |\n| 3.9 kt | 57 A | ~4.8 h | 21 | 2.9 |\n\nDecision framing to document: **range-constrained → ~2.9 kt; endurance/loiter-constrained\n→ slower (2.0 kt ≈ 13.5 h, more than double 3.5 kt); time-constrained → 3.5 kt costs ~20 %\nrange, only worth it with margin to spare.**\n\n## Scope of doc changes\n\n- [ ] Add a **\"Speed → power, endurance, range (empirical)\"** subsection to\n      `bizzyboat_power.md` with the table above + the 3-panel plot (endurance / range /\n      usage+draw vs speed).\n- [ ] Revise the **Drive efficiently** bullets to replace \"≈3–3.5 kt range-optimal\" with\n      the plateau (~2.9–3.0 kt) finding + the time-vs-range-vs-endurance trade-off.\n- [ ] Note this **partially fills #88** (PWM × current sweep) from pooled in-water data —\n      it is not a controlled bench sweep but covers the operational 2–4 kt band.\n- [ ] Commit the plot under `docs/analysis/2026-06-24/` (companion analysis dir, like the\n      06-23 one) and link it from the power doc.\n- [ ] Cross-reference #315 (distance-aware reserve), #318 (SOC estimator), #196 (idle/charger).\n\n## Provenance (local, uncommitted)\n\n`~/data/logs/analysis/2026-06-24_deployment.db` (focused power/nav SQLite extract) +\n`~/data/logs/analysis/2026-06-24/` (scripts `coulomb.py`, `speed_bins.py`,\n`speed_curve.py` and plots). Regenerable from the bag\n`~/data/logs/gabby/logs/bizzyboat/2026-06-24T12-11-05+00-00`.\n\n\u003e **Caveat to carry into the doc:** no current sensor (`BATT_MONITOR 3`); current/power\n\u003e are modeled (quadratic PWM, ±~30 %). The *relative* speed trade-offs and the\n\u003e current/Ah-based metrics are robust; absolute amps/watts carry the band. Pier\n\u003e deployments are excluded (tidal current makes SOG an unreliable stand-in for STW).\n\n---\n**Authored-By**: `Claude Code Agent`\n**Model**: `Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context)`","files":null}]}